course: dictionary
lesson: 2013
title: Learning a new word: condor
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A condor is a very large vulture with huge wings and a bald head. You can learn its meaning from the words around it. «The Andean condor has wings about 3 metres across and glides on rising air.» Wings, glides and rising air tell you that a condor is a bird that flies.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence, then test your meaning. «One was found to fly over 170 kilometres without flapping once.» A condor can fly very far without flapping its wings. If you swap in "a bird that glides" for "condor", the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning is probably right.
explain: Place names can be clues too. The Andean condor takes its name from the Andes mountains, and the California condor takes its name from California. When you meet a new word with a place in it, ask: does the name tell me where this thing lives?
fact: A condor is a very large vulture with huge wings and a bald head.
fact: «The Andean condor» lives in the Andes mountains, and «the California condor» lives in California.
fact: Condors are scavengers: they eat dead animals.
fact: «The Andean condor has wings about 3 metres across» and it can glide for hours on rising air.
fact: In 1987 there were only 27 California condors left in the world; today there are over 500, and more than half fly wild.
exercise: «The Andean condor has wings about 3 metres across and glides on rising air.» What is a condor? | A kind of fish that lives in rivers | A small songbird with a red breast | *A very large vulture with huge wings and a bald head | A mountain made of rock
exercise: The Andes mountains give the Andean condor its name. Which place is the Andean condor linked to? | The Arctic Ocean | *The Andes mountains | Scotland | Paris
exercise: «One was found to fly over 170 kilometres without flapping once.» What helps condors fly so far? | Flapping their wings hard all day | Running fast along the ground | *Gliding on rising air | Swimming in the sea
test: «Condors are scavengers and eat dead animals.» What does a scavenger eat? | Grass | Seeds | *Dead animals | Live fish
test: «In 1987 the last wild California condors were caught, leaving 27 in the world.» How many California condors were left in the world in 1987? | 3 | *27 | 500 | 170
exam: What does «soar like a condor» most likely mean? | To run very fast on the ground | To dig holes in the ground | *To rise high and float on the air without flapping | To sleep all day
